WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.400
The first film showed a field as a sum of its species.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.880
Now follow one real field for a decade.

3
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.520
How much of its extra yield is complementarity, and how much is selection?

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.920
Does the answer change as the plots age?

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.360
The field is Cedar Creek, in Minnesota.

6
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.000
In spring nineteen ninety-four, David Tilman and colleagues sowed a hundred and

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.660
sixty-eight plots there.

8
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.760
Each plot was nine metres on a side.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:37.323
Each plot got one, two, four, eight or sixteen species.

10
00:00:38.643 --> 00:00:43.343
They were drawn at random from a pool of eighteen grassland and savanna species.

11
00:00:44.823 --> 00:00:46.623
Every plot got the same seed mass.

12
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.070
Because the draw was random, nobody chose strong species for the rich plots.

13
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.290
Better soil could line up with richness only by chance.

14
00:00:57.383 --> 00:01:00.123
Every year the plants were clipped, dried and weighed.

15
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:06.103
Year after year, plots with more species generally grew more aboveground biomass.

16
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:12.967
Joseph Fargione and colleagues then split the extra yield in each year, using every

17
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:15.047
species' yield in mixture and alone.

18
00:01:16.327 --> 00:01:18.407
The green bar is complementarity.

19
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:21.027
The violet bar is selection.

20
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.710
In the early years, selection was positive.

21
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.490
Rich plots outyielded poor ones partly because they more often held a productive

22
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.490
species.

23
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.020
Then the balance shifted toward complementarity.

24
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.580
Selection shrank, crossed zero and ended strongly negative.

25
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.400
Now the biggest gains went to species that yield little alone.

26
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.460
The extra biomass came to be associated with more nitrogen held in the plants.

27
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.090
So the same plots gave a different answer depending on which years you looked at.

28
00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:00.430
Each year's split is a snapshot, and the snapshots changed.

29
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:05.363
Now zoom into the sixteen-species plots and follow their species.

30
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:11.883
On average, each species swings proportionally more from year to year than species

31
00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:12.883
in poorer plots.

32
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.427
So the parts became less steady, on average.

33
00:02:17.847 --> 00:02:21.707
Will the plot's total be more steady, or less steady, than a monoculture's?

34
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:34.833
More steady. Tilman, Peter Reich and Johannes Knops reported this in two thousand

35
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:40.843
six. The total varied proportionally less, even though its species, on average,

36
00:02:41.103 --> 00:02:42.103
varied more.

37
00:02:42.817 --> 00:02:45.637
Did opposite swings grow stronger in richer plots?

38
00:02:47.037 --> 00:02:49.537
Their analysis found no evidence of that.

39
00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:54.437
Averaging, together with a higher mean, explained the steadier total.

40
00:02:55.777 --> 00:02:59.337
A higher mean helps only if the swings grow less than in proportion.

41
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:05.170
Assigned at random and watched for a decade, richer plots grew more and grew

42
00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.110
steadier. Their species, on average, grew less steady.
